Body:
Confirming this on v1.18.16, with additional evidence that the current behavior contradicts the stated intent in the code itself — which arguably makes this a bug rather than a feature request.

In `packages/opencode/src/config/config.ts`, every config scope is merged through:

```ts
// Custom merge function that concatenates array fields instead of replacing them
// Keep remeda's deep conditional merge type out of hot config-loading paths; TS profiling showed it dominates here.
function mergeConfig(target: Info, source: Info): Info {
  return mergeDeep(target, source) as Info
}

function mergeConfigConcatArrays(target: Info, source: Info): Info {
  const merged = mergeConfig(target, source)
  if (target.instructions && source.instructions) {
    merged.instructions = Array.from(new Set([...target.instructions, ...source.instructions]))
  }
  return merged
}
```

The comment says arrays are concatenated instead of replaced, but the implementation only ever does that for `instructions`. `plugin` is concatenated and deduped through a separate mechanism (`mergePluginOrigins`). Every other array field — including `skills.urls` and `skills.paths` — falls through to remeda's `mergeDeep`, which deep-merges objects but replaces arrays wholesale.

**Reproduction (v1.18.16):**

`~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`:
```json
{ "skills": { "urls": ["https://a.example/.well-known/skills/", "https://b.example/.well-known/skills/"] } }
```

Project `opencode.json`:
```json
{ "skills": { "urls": ["https://c.example/.well-known/skills/"] } }
```

Result: only the skills from `c.example` are available in the session; both globally configured skill sources silently disappear. There is no warning, so this is easy to miss — you only notice when a global skill you rely on stops being offered inside one project.

Since skill sources are inherently additive — exactly like `plugin`, which *does* concatenate — union-merging `skills.paths`/`skills.urls` across scopes (as already done for `instructions`) looks like the behavior the code comment intended all along.
